/**
 * The embedder API. Create one per app with [`DspiCore::new`].
 *
 * Every call that touches the device blocks its thread for the transfers it
 * makes, so a binding calls these off its UI thread.
 */
public protocol DspiCoreProtocol: AnyObject, Sendable {
    
    /**
     * Write the whole cached state to the device in one bulk transfer:
     * chunked `REQ_SET_ALL_PARAMS_CHUNK` (`0xA3`, at most 2048 bytes and
     * the transport's `max_transfer` per chunk), or single-shot
     * `REQ_SET_ALL_PARAMS` (`0xA1`) over a link without chunked bulk. The
     * device applies it on its main loop once the last byte lands.
     *
     * The write is bracketed with the connection's echo filter, so the
     * `BULK_INVALIDATED` it causes is recognised as our own. On success the
     * mirror is re-decoded from the image that was sent (the codec's clamps
     * applied).
     *
     * Runs under the transport's device lock (`Transport::lock`, a no-op
     * except on DSPi Link, where another console's changes are held off
     * for its duration; PLAN "Link device lock").
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open, or, like the
     * old core, when no image has been read yet (nothing to apply: call
     * [`DspiCore::fetch_all`] first); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] when the
     * firmware speaks another wire format; [`DspiError::Locked`] /
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] when the Link hub refuses its lock (nothing is
     * sent); the transport's typed error when a transfer fails (the device
     * then applies nothing).
     */
    func applyAllParams() throws 
    
    /**
     * The device's name as last known, with no traffic, kept as
     * [`DspiCore::cached_input_source_name`] keeps source names. "" when
     * unnamed. Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without device names;
     * `Disconnected`.
     */
    func cachedDeviceName() throws  -> String
    
    /**
     * The name of input source `source` as last known, with no traffic: read
     * on connect, kept current by notifications (from any client) and by
     * [`DspiCore::set_input_source_name`], and read again after a lost
     * notification. "" when unnamed. For a client's rows, which would
     * otherwise read over the wire what connect has just read.
     *
     * Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without source names; `InvalidArgument`
     * for a source past the table; `Disconnected` without a device or before
     * it was probed.
     */
    func cachedInputSourceName(source: UInt8) throws  -> String
    
    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::redo`] has a change to apply again. False with no
     * device.
     */
    func canRedo()  -> Bool
    
    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::undo`] has a change to reverse. False with no
     * device.
     */
    func canUndo()  -> Bool
    
    /**
     * Take the live state as what the active preset holds: from now on
     * [`DspiCore::preset_changes`] lists what differs from it. The core
     * does this itself after a connect, a load, a save, a revert and a
     * factory reset. Without a device, or without an image in this build's
     * format, there is no baseline.
     */
    func captureBaseline() 
    
    /**
     * Whether enabling `output` would conflict with Core 1's mode (PDM on
     * the last output and the Core 1 EQ worker exclude each other, and the
     * firmware skips a blocked enable silently). One `REQ_GET_CORE1_CONFLICT`
     * read; a failed read is an error, never "no conflict".
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an output past the
     * device's output count (`DspState.num_output_channels`);
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device; a stall, timeout or other
     * transfer error from the read.
     */
    func checkCore1Conflict(output: UInt8) throws  -> Bool
    
    /**
     * Clear the device's sticky clip flags (`REQ_CLEAR_CLIPS`, which reads
     * the flags back and clears them; the value is not used).
     *
     * Meter housekeeping, not a parameter: it touches no setting and
     * nothing in the bulk image, so it is allowed on a read-only
     * connection too. This is the one named exception (PLAN §1.2) to
     * "state-changing opcodes only on a `Writable`": it goes out on a plain
     * `Connection`.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open; the
     * transport's typed error when the transfer fails.
     */
    func clearClips() throws 
    
    /**
     * Close the device opened by [`DspiCore::open_fd`] (the same as
     * [`DspiCore::disconnect`]); afterwards the app may close its
     * `UsbDeviceConnection`. Not called `close` because UniFFI's Kotlin
     * objects already have `close()` (free the object).
     */
    func closeFd() 
    
    /**
     * The device's configuration as a `.dspipreset` document (JSON, schema
     * 1), from the mirror: no transfer. `name` and `app_version` go into
     * `meta`; `links` is the app's input-pair link mask (bit p: pair p),
     * written as `global.inputPairLinked`.
     *
     * The document holds what the device's wire format carries: a key or
     * block that firmware lacks is left out, `crossfeed.pairMap` is written
     * only when the device reports a map over the extension channel, and
     * the upmixer only on an RP2350. A read-only device (another wire
     * format) exports what its image showed.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device;
     * [`DspiError::Transfer`] before its settings were read;
     * [`DspiError::Unsupported`] for firmware whose image this build cannot
     * map.
     */
    func configExport(name: String, appVersion: String, links: UInt8) throws  -> String
    
    /**
     * Apply a `.dspipreset` document to the device as one bulk write, under
     * the device lock. `options`: [`IMPORT_VOLUMES`] also applies the master
     * and user volume, [`IMPORT_HARDWARE`] the hardware I/O and the
     * limiters. The crossfeed pair map goes over the extension channel when
     * the device has one.
     *
     * Every setting the document holds no readable value for keeps the
     * device's value: a missing key or block, a value of the wrong type, an
     * `eq` or `crossover` with no entries. A bank with entries replaces the
     * channel's, the rest going flat. Delays past the device's delay line
     * are shortened to it. Channels the device lacks are named, not moved.
     *
     * Returns JSON. On success `{ok: true, error: "", lines, clean, links,
     * hardwareWritten, leftAlone, outOfRange}`: `lines` is the report,
     * `clean` false when anything was missing, skipped, left alone,
     * shortened or out of range, `leftAlone` the keys left alone,
     * `outOfRange` the numbers past what this device takes, each
     * `{path, value, applied}` (registry path with indices, the document's
     * value, the nearest end written instead; WP P0U's rows), `links` the input-pair link mask to
     * restore (null when the document has none), and `hardwareWritten`
     * whether I/O was changed while the device stores its output
     * configuration apart from presets (save that to keep it). Otherwise
     * `{ok: false, error}`: the text is not a document this build reads, or
     * the device could not be written (disconnected, read-only firmware,
     * another console's lock, a failed transfer); the mirror then stays as
     * it was.
     */
    func configImport(text: String, options: UInt32)  -> String
    
    /**
     * The open connection's generation: unique per connection and never
     * reused while the process runs, so it changes on every reopen and on
     * a Link re-bind. 0 when nothing is open.
     */
    func connectionGeneration()  -> UInt64
    
    /**
     * Copy the live state to `dest` and save it to `source` again, so
     * `source` stays the active preset (the Mac console's "Copy to"). Each
     * save waits for the device; one device lock covers both.
     *
     * If the save to `dest` is not confirmed, nothing more is sent and the
     * baseline stays. After the second save, the live state is the
     * baseline.
     *
     * Errors: the first failing save's (`Refused` with its `PRESET_*`
     * status); [`DspiError::Transfer`] when `dest`'s save was not
     * confirmed; otherwise as [`DspiCore::save_preset`].
     */
    func copyPreset(source: UInt8, dest: UInt8) throws 
    
    /**
     * Whether enabling output `output` would collide on Core 1, and if so
     * why, in the words [`DspiCore::write_param`] refuses it with: `None`
     * when it would not. The device decides
     * ([`DspiCore::check_core1_conflict`]); the words come from the layout.
     *
     * Errors: those of [`DspiCore::check_core1_conflict`].
     */
    func core1ConflictReason(output: UInt8) throws  -> String?
    
    /**
     * Close the flow: the user left. No traffic. `InvalidArgument` during a
     * run, which goes on until ended.
     */
    func coreVregCalClose() throws 
    
    /**
     * Save the voltage that passed, with a `SET`: the one explicit step from
     * a pass to flash. The status is read first, so a hang since the last
     * poll takes the pass back. Read back by later polls: `Committed` or
     * `VerifyFailed`. `typed` is the word when the view's `commit_needs_word`
     * says so, even if it was typed for the trial. A refusal leaves the flow in
     * `Passed`, with the trial running. Errors as for starting.
     */
    func coreVregCalCommit(typed: String?) throws 
    
    /**
     * Keep a trial found on the device: the soak counts from now, for
     * `soak_s` seconds. A trial outside the flow's range is left alone and
     * cannot pass. Sends nothing. Errors as for opening.
     */
    func coreVregCalKeep(soakS: UInt32) throws 
    
    /**
     * Open the flow: the user reached it, from Advanced. Shows the warning
     * and what is on record; sends no write.
     *
     * `Disconnected`; `Unsupported` without a usable `EXT_CORE_VREG`;
     * `Denied` after a hub refused a write on this connection; `ReadOnly`;
     * `InvalidArgument` during a run.
     */
    func coreVregCalOpen() throws 
    
    /**
     * Read the device and advance the flow; never fails, the view says.
     * Call on every connect and about once a second while the page shows.
     * The only write that can go out is the next step of a run the user
     * started; never a `SET`, and none after the connection changed. Blocks
     * for a read: call it off the UI thread.
     */
    func coreVregCalPoll()  -> VregCalView
    
    /**
     * Start a run: the current record is cleared, `target` goes on trial
     * once the device shows that, and the soak counts for `soak_s` seconds.
     * `typed` is what the user typed when the view says this needs the word.
     *
     * `OvervoltWordRequired`, with no write, if it does and that is not
     * exactly the word. `InvalidArgument`, with no write, for a voltage
     * outside the build default to 1.25 V, a soak under the minimum, a trial
     * already running, or the wrong phase. `Disconnected`,
     * `Unsupported`, `ReadOnly`; `Refused`, `Denied`, `Locked`, `Transfer` for
     * the write, which is not retried.
     */
    func coreVregCalStart(target: UInt8, soakS: UInt32, typed: String?) throws 
    
    /**
     * The calibration flow as it stands, from memory: no traffic. It waits
     * for a poll or request in flight, so a slow link can hold it up as long
     * as that read: call it off the UI thread too.
     */
    func coreVregCalView()  -> VregCalView
    
    /**
     * Play an event on the demo device, for clients' tests and demo mode.
     * `seconds` is for [`VregDemoEvent::AdvanceUptime`]. `Unsupported`, with
     * no traffic, on anything but a simulated device.
     */
    func coreVregDemoEvent(event: VregDemoEvent, seconds: UInt32) throws 
    
    /**
     * Manual `AUTO`: the firmware's automatic raising after repeated hangs.
     * Errors as for starting.
     */
    func coreVregManualAuto(on: Bool) throws 
    
    /**
     * Manual `CLEAR`: forget the hang count and the last trial failure.
     * `InvalidArgument` during a run, whose result it would erase. Errors
     * otherwise as for starting.
     */
    func coreVregManualClear() throws 
    
    /**
